# puml — Cursor authoring rules

This project renders diagrams with [`puml`](https://github.com/grahambrooks/puml),
a native PlantUML-compatible CLI. Apply these rules when editing `.puml` /
`.plantuml` files.

## Files

- One diagram per file, wrapped in `@startuml` / `@enduml`.
- Filenames: lowercase, hyphenated, intent-first (`signup-sequence.puml`).

## Render

```bash
puml diagram.puml -o diagram.svg          # SVG (preferred)
puml diagram.puml -o diagram.svg --watch  # live reload
```

## Diagram selection

| Intent                          | Diagram   |
|---------------------------------|-----------|
| Interactions over time          | Sequence  |
| Static structure / inheritance  | Class     |
| Branching activity              | Activity  |
| State machine                   | State     |
| User goals                      | Use Case  |
| Software architecture           | C4        |
| Infrastructure                  | Deployment|

## C4 model

```plantuml
@startuml
!include <C4/C4_Container>
title <System> — Containers

Person(user, "User")
System_Boundary(svc, "<System>") {
  Container(api, "API", "<runtime>")
  ContainerDb(db, "Database", "<engine>")
}
Rel(user, api, "Uses", "HTTPS")
Rel(api, db, "Reads / writes", "SQL")
@enduml
```

Canonical includes only: `<C4/C4_Context>`, `<C4/C4_Container>`,
`<C4/C4_Component>`, `<C4/C4_Deployment>`, `<C4/C4_Dynamic>`.

## Style

- Stable aliases for every node.
- `title` directly after `@startuml`.
- Declarations first, `Rel(...)` calls last.
- Parents above children in class hierarchies.

## Don't

- Don't mix multiple diagram types in one file.
- Don't use sprites, OpenIconic, salt mockups, or `LAYOUT_*` hints — `puml`
  ignores them.
- Don't hand-edit generated `.svg` / `.png` files.
